WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.775
Two web servers run the same playbook.

2
00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:08.708
Each needs a greeting, and the two should be allowed to differ a little.

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.763
But a variable can be set in many places.

4
00:00:11.763 --> 00:00:13.419
The docs list 22 levels.

5
00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:17.145
In practice you need seven, from weakest to strongest.

6
00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.122
Start at the bottom.

7
00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:22.172
The role's defaults file says: Hello, DevOps!

8
00:00:22.172 --> 00:00:26.171
That is only the author's suggestion, so it is the weakest.

9
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.927
Next, the inventory.

10
00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:35.181
The file group_vars/web/vars.yml sets the greeting for the whole web group: Hello from Ansible.

11
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.402
It sits higher, so it wins on both servers.

12
00:00:38.402 --> 00:00:40.288
The default is still there.

13
00:00:40.288 --> 00:00:41.265
It just loses.

14
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:46.689
Now host_vars/web2.yml gives web2, the canary, its own text.

15
00:00:46.689 --> 00:00:48.691
A host beats its group.

16
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.661
It names only web2, so web1 keeps the group's value.

17
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:55.977
What you type for one run beats everything.

18
00:00:55.977 --> 00:01:00.027
Add -e with Hi from -e, and both hosts print Hi from -e.

19
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:02.158
But only for that one command.

20
00:01:02.158 --> 00:01:05.751
Run again without it, and the inventory values are back.

21
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:08.066
So how do you know which one won?

22
00:01:08.066 --> 00:01:10.611
Ansible never asks you, and never warns.

23
00:01:10.611 --> 00:01:13.347
It picks the winner by this list, silently.

24
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.844
So you ask each host what it sees.

25
00:01:15.844 --> 00:01:19.529
The debug module prints a variable after all the rules.

26
00:01:19.529 --> 00:01:22.141
Here, exactly the two values on screen.

27
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:24.754
One level breaks the pattern.

28
00:01:24.754 --> 00:01:29.756
Role vars, in the role's vars folder, sit above group_vars and host_vars.

29
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:33.749
Put the greeting there by mistake, and it beats the whole inventory.

30
00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:36.119
Now change host_vars for web2.

31
00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:39.420
Nothing happens, and Ansible gives no warning.

32
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:46.552
So defaults is for every setting a user may want to change, and vars is for the role's own fixed details.

33
00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:49.937
Where does the winning value go?

34
00:01:49.937 --> 00:01:51.428
Often into a template.

35
00:01:51.428 --> 00:01:54.955
The template module reads a Jinja2 file on your Mac.

36
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.570
Double curly braces print a value.

37
00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.913
Here, the greeting.

38
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:05.305
It fills the file in for each host, with that host's values, and copies the result to the server.

39
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:07.029
One file, two results.

40
00:02:07.029 --> 00:02:11.925
Templates render on the control node, so the servers do not need Jinja2.

41
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:20.664
Put knobs in role defaults, shared values in group_vars, exceptions in host_vars, and one-off overrides in -e.

42
00:02:20.664 --> 00:02:24.793
Set each value in one place, and ask Ansible what it sees.
